      "text": "Let $x = k\\sin \\alpha$ and $y = k\\cos \\alpha,$ where $k > 0$ and $0 < \\alpha < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} &= \\frac{(k \\sin \\alpha + k \\cos \\alpha)^2}{k^2 \\sin^2 \\alpha + k^2 \\cos^2 \\alpha} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k^2 (\\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\alpha)^2}{k^2 (\\sin^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha)} \\\\\n&= (\\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\alpha)^2 \\\\\n&= \\sin^2 \\alpha + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha \\\\\n&= 1 + \\sin 2\\alpha.\n\\end{align*}Note that $0 < 2\\alpha < \\pi,$ so $0 < \\sin 2\\alpha \\le 1.$  Therefore,\n\\[1 + \\sin 2\\alpha \\le 2,\\]so the maximum value is $\\boxed{2}.$The answer is: 2",
