      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we can start by simplifying the expression. Let's expand the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can split this into three separate fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\). To do this, we can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the following form:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2)\\left(1^2 + \\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)^2\\right) \\geq (x + y)^2.\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2)\\left(1 + \\frac{y^2}{x^2}\\right) \\geq (x + y)^2,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2)\\left(1 + \\frac{y^2}{x^2}\\right) \\geq (x^2 + y^2),\n\\]\n\nor:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\frac{y^2}{x^2} \\geq 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is always true. However, it doesn't directly help us find the maximum value. Instead, let's consider the equality case of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, which occurs when \\(\\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{y}{x}\\), i.e., \\(x = y\\). If \\(x = y\\), then:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{2x^2}{x^2 + x^2} = \\frac{2x^2}{2x^2} = 1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\) is 1, and the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n1 + 1 = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum value is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
