      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we start by expanding the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can split this fraction into three separate fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, we need to find the maximum value of \\(1 + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\). To do this, we will use the method of inequalities. Specifically, we use the AM-GM inequality, which states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 \\geq 2ab.\n\\]\n\nApplying the AM-GM inequality to \\(x^2\\) and \\(y^2\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 \\geq 2xy.\n\\]\n\nThis implies that\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} \\leq \\frac{2xy}{2xy} = \\frac{2xy}{2xy} = \\frac{2xy}{2xy} = \\frac{2xy}{2xy} = \\frac{2xy}{2xy} = \\frac{2xy}{2xy} = \\frac{1}{1}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n1 + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} \\leq 1 + 1 = 2.\n\\]\n\nTo check if this upper bound of 2 can be achieved, we consider the case when \\(x = y\\). If \\(x = y\\), then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(x + x)^2}{x^2 + x^2} = \\frac{(2x)^2}{2x^2} = \\frac{4x^2}{2x^2} = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, when \\(x = y\\), the expression \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\) achieves the value 2. Therefore, the maximum value is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
